 @Demon09-_-  Yeah, the 6500xt and 6400 were originally laptop GPUs, though I don't know if they share the exact same silicon as the laptop variant (in which case AMD would have had chips on hand already) or if they were their own run / customized (if this is the case then AMD had a completed GPU design w/ minor mods needed to make a desktop version).  Regardless of the exact circumstances, they had a design that could clock exceptionally high when removed from the thermal and power limits of a laptop, could hit discreet budget segment performance, and actually be available at the target $200 MSRP.  Basically they were a reaction to the crazy market conditions, and I think time to market was the biggest driver in their release rather than cost.  The narrative that AMD cut costs by going w/ pcie 4x ignores the origin of the GPU's design; they didn't cut anything given those features were never on the GPU to begin with.

Put another way, changing the design from 4x to 8x, adding encoders, etc would have increased how long it took to get the cards out the door by adding months to the design and associated manufacturing steps versus leveraging what they already had on hand / mostly completed to address the non-existent budget price bracket.  Remember, the market at the time had $400 1650 Supers and $350 used 580s; that's what the 6500xt launched against (IIRC the 5500 was also insanely priced but I don't remember the specifics of that card).  Even at their worst pricing, cards were consistently available at $270 right after launch, so about $80 cheaper than used GPUs in roughly the same performance class.  It's something that bugged me about the launch, nearly every major site compared the 6500xt against the market two years before its release, not what it actually launched against.  For budget gamers, it was far more sanely priced than just about anything else available new or used, unless you got really lucky scouring used listings.  At $160, I'd argue it's still pretty well situated compared to other budget priced GPUs available today.

I'd like to see the lower end of the market get serviced more quickly this time around, even if it means leveraging a GPU that has roots in the mobile segment again.  My hope would be AMD keeping this approach in mind from the beginning so they avoid the shortcomings of the 6500xt release (ie have the GPU ready to use 4x or 8x lanes depending on where it's used).  I think it's a sound idea for keeping the sub $200 discreet market alive, and I just don't see nvidia bothering with that space.

